#: input/pagecontent/scenarios.md:62
msgid "J2a. On a routine visit to Village 04, Vilay finds a newborn the Lao system does not know exists. Her phone has had no signal for weeks. Khamla shows the two cards. The CHW app reads them with read-unverified (<a0>STT-VER-011</a0>; <a1>STT-B45-002</a1>, <a2>STT-ZIP-002</a2>, <a3>STT-CBR-003</a3>), which needs no trust list. It shows the doses, but checks no signature and saves nothing yet."
msgstr ""

#: input/pagecontent/scenarios.md:68
#, python-brace-format
msgid "J2b. On the CHW app's newborn form (structured data capture, {code0}) she adds Somsack to the Sengmany household ({code1}), with a temporary id. Bounmy confirms the details as head of household. The app saves the doses read from the cards as reported by the family, not verified ({code2}, {code3}: {code4} false). Vilay refers Somsack to the district health center ({code5}). Everything stays queued on the phone, for 28 days if need be, and syncs when it next reaches a signal."
msgstr ""

#: input/pagecontent/scenarios.md:84
msgid "J3 — Made official (Vientiane District Health Center, 4 weeks later)"
msgstr ""

#: input/pagecontent/scenarios.md:88
#, python-brace-format
msgid "J3a. Nurse Chanthasone opens the referral and scans the QRs. The EHR verifies each DVC offline against that copy (<a0>STT-VER-001</a0>…<a1>009</a1>, <a2>STT-KID-002</a2>), without contacting Thailand. Both verify, so it imports them as FHIR {code0}s ({code1}, {code2}) with provenance ({code3}; <a3>STT-VER-007</a3>, <a4>STT-VER-008</a4>), replacing the reported doses of J2. If a DVC fails (a tampered card, say), the EHR names the step and the reason, the doses stay unverified, and the nurse decides by national catch-up policy."
msgstr ""

#: input/pagecontent/scenarios.md:97
#, python-brace-format
msgid "J3b. The Thai Hep B product matches nothing on the national schedule. The EHR keeps Thailand's product coding and adds the schedule coding beside it, from the national map ({code0}). For the Hep B product the map has no entry, so Nurse Chanthasone makes the clinical call that it is the schedule's birth dose ({code1}), and her decision is added to the map for the next clinic. The schedule ({code2}) then runs: the birth doses count, and pentavalent 1, bOPV 1 and PCV 1 are due at 6 weeks ({code3}). Khamla leaves with the date ({code4}). Over the year Somsack gets those doses and measles-rubella at 9 months, in Lao PDR."
msgstr ""

